  "id": 218911597,
  "iid": 10403,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "SSHv2: Add support for reading shared secret from keylog file",
  "description": "As of now, the ssh dissector supports reading the ephemeral private key of either the client or server and use it to compute the shared secret of a connection. The shared secret in turn is then used by the dissector to decrypt encrypted packets (similar to TLS keylog file). However, there is currently no option to provide the dissector with the shared secret directly. This causes multiple issues:\n\n- Connections which use new and not yet implemented key exchange types (like the hybrid post-quantum key exchange `sntrup761x25519-sha512@openssh.com`, the new default of OpenSSH) can not be decrypted even if the shared secret is known. Allowing one to supply the dissector with the shared secret directly allows it to bypass the key exchange calculations (and therefore the issue of unimplemented methods).\n- OpenSSH does not log the ephemeral private key to console, even when compiling with certain debug flags (`-DDEBUG_KEX=1 -DDEBUG_KEXDH=1 -DDEBUG_KEXECDH=1`). Therefore, obtaining the private key for testing purposes from an OpenSSH server / client requires one to patch the source code before compiling. In contrast, the shared secret of the connection is being logged to console when compiled with the debug flags set.\n\nThe current keylog format for the ssh dissector is as follows:\n\n`<hex-encoded cookie> <hex-encoded private key>`\n\nwhere the cookie is the random byte value included as part of the SSH_MSG_KEXINIT message.\n\nThis PR changes this format to:\n\n`<hex-encoded cookie> <PRIVATE_KEY|SHARED_SECRET> <hex-encoded key material>`\n\nwhere the middle part is a static label indicating the type of key material that follows. Setting the label to `PRIVATE_KEY` will make the ssh dissector behave as before, calculating the shared secret on its own. When the label is set to `SHARED_SECRET` the computation is being skipped and the provided key material is directly being used as the shared secret for key derivation. For backwards compatibility I kept support for the old format as well.",
  "state": "merged",
